Building matiec
===============

Supported build tools
---------------------

Building from a Git checkout requires:

* Autoconf 2.69 or newer
* Automake 1.16 or newer
* Bison 2.4 or newer (Bison 3.x is used in CI)
* Flex 2.6 or newer
* GNU Make or a compatible Make implementation
* A C and C++ compiler (GCC on Linux and Clang on macOS are tested in CI)

The generated `configure` script and Makefile templates are committed, so a
release archive can be built without Autoconf or Automake. Bison and Flex are
still required when their generated parser sources need to be refreshed.

Bootstrap and build from Git
----------------------------

Run the following commands from the repository root:

    autoreconf --install
    ./configure
    make --jobs=2
    make check

`autoreconf --install` is the single supported bootstrap command. It refreshes
`configure`, every `Makefile.in`, and the helper scripts in `config/`.

Sanitizer builds
----------------

The sanitizer targets make an isolated source copy, configure it with the
requested sanitizer, build it, and run the same regression entry point:

    make check-asan
    make check-ubsan

AddressSanitizer runs with leak detection enabled. Both sanitizer targets use
an isolated source copy and execute the complete regression suite.

Test coverage
-------------

`make check` runs focused compiler tests plus sequential in-process compilation,
in-memory generator output assertions, generated-C ABI and runtime checks, CLI
tests, syntax and initialization suites, and byte-for-byte output
characterization. See `docs/architecture/compiler-boundaries.md` for the
corresponding architecture and embedding contracts.

Cross-compiling for Windows from Linux
--------------------------------------

Configure with the appropriate MinGW host tuple, then build normally:

    ./configure --host=i586-pc-mingw32
    make

For a statically linked build:

    ./configure --host=i586-pc-mingw32 LDFLAGS="-static"
    make

Maintaining generated build files
----------------------------------

After changing `configure.ac` or any `Makefile.am`, run:

    autoreconf --force --install
    ./configure
    make --jobs=2
    make check

Use `make distclean` to remove configured build products. Generated build files
must be refreshed with the supported toolchain and committed with the source
change that required them.
